From dbf9bb3e26e6fe3b66ad1851d705244a3d221c7e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qiyinxi <qiyinxi@foxmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 20:50:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 50/65] media/vpx: return textures of unfetched frames when
 destroying

In GPU texture mode (the CUDA decoder with
ANDROID_EMU_MEDIA_DECODER_CUDA_VPX=1 and the host GLES renderer) every
decoded VP8/VP9 frame holds a Y/UV texture pair taken from the
decoder's MediaTexturePool. fetchAllFrames() moves the frames into
mSnapshotHelper after every decode. When the guest fetches a frame,
getImage() swaps its pair into the color buffer and puts the pair that
comes back into the pool. Frames that the guest has not fetched when
it destroys the decoder are dropped together with mSnapshotHelper, and
their pairs with them: the pool's cleanUpTextures(), which
~MediaHostRenderer() runs, only deletes pairs that are back in the
pool, so these textures stay allocated until the GL share group goes
away.

The H.264 and HEVC decoders hand such textures back in
destroyH264Context() and destroyHevcContext(); destroyVpxContext()
only deinitializes the video helper. Do the same there: return the
pairs of the frames still queued and let the pool delete them.

The guest's goldfish VPX component fetches at most one frame after
each decode, and none when it cannot get an output buffer, so frames
are left over when it is released after such a failed fetch, or
between a decode and its fetch, for example when the app that decodes
is killed.

Test: Windows 11 host, NVIDIA GPU, -gpu host,
      ANDROID_EMU_MEDIA_DECODER_CUDA_VPX=1; a guest decodes a 1080p VP9
      clip with c2.goldfish.vp9.decoder to an ImageReader, stops taking
      output after 30 frames and exits without releasing the codec.
      Without this change every destroyVpxContext() a debugger watched
      found a frame with textures still queued and returned nothing,
      and the process GPU local usage grew by about 3.4 MB, one pair,
      per session (318 to 533 MB from session 5 to 75); with it, the
      queued pairs (1 to 3 per session) went back to the pool and local
      usage stayed at 294-304 MB over 100 sessions. H.264 sessions of
      the same kind leave nothing queued and stayed flat either way.
Test: decodes killed after 1 s left 0 to 2 textured frames queued;
      without this change none came back, with it all of them did.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:Claude-Opus-5.5
Change-Id: I9345f28624cf93fdfd26a694fc32e0f97c643730
---
 .../android/emulation/MediaVpxDecoderGeneric.cpp | 16 ++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+)

diff --git a/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaVpxDecoderGeneric.cpp b/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaVpxDecoderGeneric.cpp
index ca4f4b20b..74dd899bb 100644
--- a/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaVpxDecoderGeneric.cpp
+++ b/android/emu/media/src/android/emulation/MediaVpxDecoderGeneric.cpp
@@ -359,6 +359,22 @@ void MediaVpxDecoderGeneric::flush(void* ptr) {
 
 void MediaVpxDecoderGeneric::destroyVpxContext(void* ptr) {
     VPX_DPRINT("calling destroy context");
+    // Frames decoded to GPU textures that the guest has not fetched yet still
+    // hold a Y/UV texture pair from the pool. Hand the pairs back, as the
+    // H.264 and HEVC decoders do, so that the pool deletes them; dropping the
+    // frames would leak the textures. Frames decoded in software carry no
+    // texture names.
+    while (MediaSnapshotState::FrameInfo* pFrame =
+                   mSnapshotHelper.frontFrame()) {
+        if (pFrame->texture.size() >= 2 && pFrame->texture[0] > 0 &&
+            pFrame->texture[1] > 0) {
+            mRenderer.putTextureFrame(
+                    TextureFrame{pFrame->texture[0], pFrame->texture[1]});
+        }
+        mSnapshotHelper.discardFrontFrame();
+    }
+    mRenderer.cleanUpTextures();
+
     if (mVideoHelper != nullptr) {
         mVideoHelper->deInit();
         mVideoHelper.reset(nullptr);
